  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Since May 2010, the city of Nice has created 14 play areas for dogs, spread across different neighbourhoods: spaces where dogs can run off-leash under their owner's control and responsibility, easy to spot thanks to a dedicated sign. The Nice Côte d'Azur tourist office describes them as fenced areas, also called "caniparks".</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to the "Avoir un chien en ville" page on nice.fr, the play areas are open all year, with dogs off-leash under their owner's responsibility. Seven are open 24/7: Jardin Paul Scoffier (179 bd Napoléon III, 916 m²), Jardin de la Batterie du Cimetière Russe (55-57 av. Raoul Dufy, 605 m²), Jardin avenue de la Pastorelle (290 m²), Jardin Joseph Kessel (200 m²), Saint Lambert (98 av. Saint Lambert, 107 m²), Jardin Joseph Pisano (236 bd du Mont-Boron, 191 m²) and Cessole (123 bd de Cessole, 350 m²).</p>
      <p>The other seven have opening hours: 8:30am-6pm from 1 October to 31 March, 8:30am-8pm from 1 April to 30 September.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Parc Carol de Roumanie</strong> — 27-29 avenue de Fabron, 1,416 m², the largest, drinking fountain nearby.</li>
        <li><strong>Jardin de la Villa Ratti</strong> — 1-3 avenue Ratti, 1,100 m², drinking fountain.</li>
        <li><strong>Parc du Castel des Deux Rois</strong> — 32 avenue du Mont Alban, 812 m².</li>
        <li><strong>Parc Chambrun</strong> (square Jean-Baptiste Carpeaux) — 14 avenue Chateaubriand, 700 m², in two parts: big dogs and small dogs.</li>
        <li><strong>Parc du Ray</strong> — avenue du Ray, 380 m², drinking fountain.</li>
        <li><strong>Jardin Léon Tesseire</strong> — corner of avenue de l'Olivetto / avenue George V, 305 m².</li>
        <li><strong>Parc Docteur Jean Guillaud</strong> (parc de la Clua) — rue Louis Bilbaut, 226 m².</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The city also provides "canisites" at the Nouveau Jardin Comte de Falicon (boulevard Paul Rémond - boulevard Comte de Falicon), the Jardin Alsace Lorraine (30-32 boulevard Gambetta) and place Wilson. They are open 24/7, except the Jardin Alsace Lorraine and place Wilson, which follow the opening hours of the play areas.</p>
      <h2>Rules outside the play areas</h2>
      <p>Dogs on a leash can enter all parks and gardens of the city of Nice, except children's gardens and special parks. Four sites are closed to them (guide and assistance dogs excepted): the Monastère de Cimiez garden, the Villa Masséna garden, the botanical garden and Parc Phoenix. Municipal order no. 2022-03366 states that animals must stay on the paths and on a leash, that lawns, shrub areas, flower beds, pools, water mirrors and fountains are off-limits to them, that type 2 dogs must be muzzled and held on a leash by an adult, and that category 1 dogs are banned from all parks and gardens. Children's playgrounds are closed to animals, even on a leash. Droppings must be picked up on pain of a fine.</p>
      <p>The city also provides two beaches open to dogs all year: La Lanterne beach (400 promenade des Anglais, behind the Carras harbour) and Lenval beach (promenade des Anglais, nos. 137 to 147).</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: Parc Chambrun is handy if you have a small dog who isn't comfortable around bigger ones, thanks to its separate zone.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>These parks are spread across several neighbourhoods of Nice (Fabron, Cimiez, Mont-Boron, the centre), convenient wherever you stay in the city.</p>
